   164	OLD_SA=slot2-signer@$PROJECT.iam.gserviceaccount.com              # the 2026-10-14 instrument's runtime account
   165	
   166	# SG-03: absent is the only tolerated failure. gone CMD... succeeds iff CMD reports that the thing does not exist.
   167	gone() { local out; if out=$("$@" 2>&1); then return 1; fi; grep -qiE 'not.?found|cannot find|does not exist' <<<"$out"; }
   168	die() { echo "retire-instrument: $*" >&2; exit 1; }
   169	
   170	retire_instrument() {
   171	  say "retire the 2026-10-14 instrument: its job reads another queue and can also cause $KEY signatures"
   172	  gone $GC scheduler jobs describe slot2-signer-tick --location=$REGION \
   173	    || $GC scheduler jobs delete slot2-signer-tick --location=$REGION
   174	  gone $GC run jobs describe slot2-signer --region=$REGION || $GC run jobs delete slot2-signer --region=$REGION
   175	  for role in roles/cloudkms.signer roles/cloudkms.publicKeyViewer; do
   176	    if $GC kms keys get-iam-policy $KEY --keyring=$KEYRING --location=$REGION --format=json | grep -q "$OLD_SA"; then
   177	      $GC kms keys remove-iam-policy-binding $KEY --keyring=$KEYRING --location=$REGION \
   178	        --member="serviceAccount:$OLD_SA" --role=$role >/dev/null
   179	    fi
   180	  done
   181	  # Disabling the account ends every route through it at once, including grants inherited from the project.
   182	  gone $GC iam service-accounts describe "$OLD_SA" || $GC iam service-accounts disable "$OLD_SA"
   183	
   184	  say "verify: nothing of the instrument can still sign"
   185	  gone $GC scheduler jobs describe slot2-signer-tick --location=$REGION || die "scheduler slot2-signer-tick remains"
   186	  gone $GC run jobs describe slot2-signer --region=$REGION || die "job slot2-signer remains"
   187	  ! $GC kms keys get-iam-policy $KEY --keyring=$KEYRING --location=$REGION --format=json | grep -q "$OLD_SA" \
   188	    || die "$OLD_SA still bound on $KEY"
   189	  ! $GC projects get-iam-policy $PROJECT --format=json | grep -q "$OLD_SA" || die "$OLD_SA still bound on $PROJECT"
   190	  gone $GC iam service-accounts describe "$OLD_SA" \
   191	    || [ "$($GC iam service-accounts describe "$OLD_SA" --format='value(disabled)')" = "True" ] \
   192	    || die "$OLD_SA is not disabled"
   193	  say "instrument retired and verified"
   194	}
   195	
   196	run_now() { $GC run jobs execute $JOB --region=$REGION --wait; }
   197	
   198	case "${1:-}" in
   199	  sas|keyiam|registry|build|role|job|scheduler|harden) "$1" ;;
   200	  run-now) run_now ;;
   201	  retire-instrument) retire_instrument ;;
   202	  *) echo "usage: $0 sas|role|keyiam|registry|build|job|run-now|scheduler|harden|retire-instrument"; exit 2 ;;
   203	esac
     1	# V1 clarifications after the freeze
     2	
     3	> The V1 profile is frozen inside the signed spec object (`formal/freeze-v1/FREEZE-RECORD.md`, opening commit
     4	> `52ee179`). Frozen text is not edited. This log records owner readings of frozen text that Phase 1a needs, each
     5	> dated, sourced and labelled. A reading may narrow or clarify; it never widens a claim. The next spec version
     6	> absorbs the readings, or rejects them. Where a reading and the frozen text truly conflict, the frozen text governs
     7	> and the conflict goes to the author.
     8	
     9	## CL-01 — §15.9 "reproduces … exit status" means zero versus non-zero (2026-10-26, OWNER, CLARIFY)
    10	
    11	The acceptance rule turns only on success (exit 0) versus failure. A distribution `gpgv` conforms if, on every
    12	release-format vector (`service/release-vectors/`), it returns zero exactly where upstream GnuPG 2.4.4 does. Exact
    13	non-zero codes are recorded but do not decide conformance. Evidence: both builds agree on all nine vectors, exact
    14	codes included (`service/release-vectors/RESULTS.txt`).
    15	
    16	## CL-02 — `gpgv` exit 0 is necessary, not sufficient (2026-10-26, OWNER, CLARIFY)
    17	
    18	§15.9 R3/R5: acceptance also needs the signing key's primary identity to be the R5-authenticated production key. A
    19	TEST key in the keyring makes `gpgv` exit 0 (vector 09), and the release is still refused. A valid TEST signature is
    20	accepted only on the explicit TEST provisioning path of R4(a) (vector 02), never in production mode.
    21	
    22	## CL-03 — "bounded local refusal" (A9.2 after-exit list) (2026-10-27, OWNER, NARROW-CLAIM, contestable)
    23	
    24	**Text.** A9.2 lists "bounded local refusal" for 20 Nov (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:214-215`) and defines it
    25	nowhere. A9.7 row 12 (`:476`) replaces A3's refusal-record decomposition (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md:572-581`: a
    26	portable record, a public commitment, and delivery and publication states) with "Bounded local refusal returned
    27	by the API", class **C**: a capability not offered by V1. A9.3 (`:238`) moves the portable form to the later
    28	"Portable refusal records".
    29	
    30	**Reading.** In V1 a refusal is *local*: it is returned by the API to the requesting credential and kept as
    31	operator state. It is not a signed or portable record, and it carries no commitment and no publication. It is
    32	*bounded* in three ways:
    33	
    34	1. **Closed reasons.** Refusals are typed from a fixed set: REFUSED with `TOO_LARGE`, `OUTSIDE_WINDOW`, `CLOSING`,
    35	   `SIGNING_UNAVAILABLE` or `BAD_REQUEST`, and FAILED with a stated reason. Nothing else is said about a refused
    36	   request.
    37	2. **Bounded in time.** Every accepted request ends COMPLETE or FAILED within 24 hours of acceptance (S4), within
    38	   the stated service clock tolerance (`docs/decisions/2026-10-27-service-clock-tolerance.md`).
    39	3. **Bounded in scope.** By A9's issuer invariant, a refusal never concerns a shipped receipt
    40	   (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:165`).
    41	
    42	**Evidence.** The issuer's state and refusal tests (`service/issuer/tests/`) show it locally. The E10 issuing
    43	vectors show it again when they run against the deployed API. A refusal claims nothing to anyone but the requester,
    44	and the contract says so (`docs/V1-CONTRACT.md` §3).
    45	
    46	**Why it is a reading.** "Bounded" has three plausible senses, and this takes all three, which is the narrowest
    47	claim. If the author meant something else, the frozen text governs and the question goes to him.
     1	# V1: success definition and schedule
     2	
     3	> **Dates ACCEPTED by the author, 2026-10-09** (*"I'll accept those dates"*). The success definition below is the
     4	> owner instance's revision of a Codex draft. It goes into the 16 October amendment for adoption, and is not
     5	> adopted yet. Owner instance: Claude Opus 5.5.
     6	
     7	## Success definition (to be adopted by amendment by 16 Oct)
     8	
     9	V1 succeeds when:
    10	
    11	1. **An organisation can obtain a receipt**, at a stated cost and latency, asserting that *these exact bytes were
    12	   presented, signed by key K whose authority is evidenced by named channels, no later than time T*.
    13	2. **A relying party can verify that claim** without Tessera running and without the authors' help. From V1's
    14	   documentation alone, it reaches the safe decision on every case in the prototype's scenario set, including
    15	   the cases whose answer is "not established".
    16	3. **V1 ships by 4 December 2026**, or the project stops pursuing V1 by explicit decision at that point.
    17	
    18	## Milestones (Fridays) — ceilings, not targets
    19	
    20	The author, 2026-10-09: *"nothing says we can't end before those dates."* Each date is the latest a milestone
    21	may land. Finishing early is the expected case, not a bonus.
    22	
    23	| No later than | Done means |
    24	|---|---|
    25	| 16 Oct | V1 definition and claim adopted. Band 0 re-scoped by amendment to the formal foundation for V1's claims, with design-level guarantees registered as future obligations. |
    26	| 30 Oct | V1 profile frozen. P8 encoding settled (prose proof plus golden vectors). Spec fixes landed: malleability, issuer-name binding, anchor-time rule, revocation "not evaluated by the verifier", verifier-fixed required set. The DNS channel works for the issuer's domain. |
    27	| 20 Nov | V1 issuing on AWS through an API (KMS + GPG). Model-derived conformance vectors run against it. |
    28	| 4 Dec | Relying-party test (non-expert integrator, documentation only) and dead-service test (Tessera off; A9:216. Renamed 2026-10-19 by the owner: V1 tests exit survival, not an 80-year horizon; `docs/decisions/2026-10-19-horizon-cost.md`) pass. **Ship or stop: the author's decision.** |
    29	
    30	**Slip rule.** If a milestone slips by more than a week, it is not extended quietly. The owner runs the tail
    31	procedure on what is blocking it and brings the author one choice: cut scope or stop.
    32	
    33	**Budget.** The author's time: about 2 h a week and at most 8 load-bearing rulings in total. Tokens: within the
    34	subscription, tiered Sonnet core / Opus advisor / Haiku grunt, no Fable unless justified. AWS: a small monthly
    35	cap, set by the author.
    36	
    37	> **Correction, 2026-10-27 (owner).** The budget paragraph was the owner instance's planning figure. It was never
    38	> put to the author, and the author did not accept it with the dates: *"I didn't even know there was a ruling
    39	> budget before this."* It does not bind. The owner still routes only load-bearing decisions to the author. The
    40	> count of eight is withdrawn; earlier documents that cite it (`V1-DERIVATION-DRAFT.md:277` and the reviews that
    41	> quote it) are left as written, because they are history.
    42	
    43	## What "ship" means on 4 December — OWNER PROPOSAL, 2026-10-27
    44	
    45	The author asked the owner to propose this. He recalled that at the start he had pictured *"a usable endpoint
    46	someone can call"*. Shipping V1 means all of the following:
    47	
    48	1. **A usable endpoint.** `POST /v1/requests` on AWS (API Gateway and Lambda, as built in `service/issuer/`), with
    49	   both real signers. Clients are invited and get an API key. V1 is free (the S7 recommendation), the content
    50	   limit is 1 MiB, and there is no availability target (S8).
    51	2. **A public verifier release**, signed with the release key on the author's YubiKey, in the live-boot ceremony
    52	   on the System76 machine (`docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md`). This is the custody gate the author ruled on
    53	   2026-10-18. It takes one session with the author, planned for the week of 16 November.
    54	3. **A service page on wamason.com.** It states the claim, the §3 limits of `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md` (including the
    55	   case where both channels are captured), how to call the endpoint, and how to verify without us.
    56	4. **The two 4 Dec tests pass.**
    57	   - *Relying party:* a model from a family that has not worked on Tessera or tupay's T1 gets only the published
    58	     documentation and must reach the safe verdict on every scenario. If the author knows a human integrator
    59	     willing to try, their run counts as well, and more.
    60	   - *Dead service:* on a clean Azure VM, with the AWS endpoint switched off, receipts are verified using only the
    61	     public release and the receipts (`docs/decisions/2026-10-27-v1-signer-placement.md` §4).
    62	
    63	Ship or stop stays the author's decision. This list says what he would be deciding on.

