   107	# main.tf, data "aws_iam_policy_document" "signer_assume": for_each over
   108	#   setunion(keys(var.signer_principals), keys(var.signer_web_identities)), and for a slot in signer_web_identities:
   109	statement {
   110	  actions = ["sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"]
   111	  principals {
   112	    type        = "Federated"
   113	    identifiers = ["accounts.google.com"]
   114	  }
   115	  condition {
   116	    test     = "StringEquals"
   117	    variable = "accounts.google.com:sub"
   118	    values   = [var.signer_web_identities[each.key].sub]
   119	  }
   120	  condition {
   121	    test     = "StringEquals"
   122	    variable = "accounts.google.com:aud"          # for Google tokens AWS maps this key to azp (the account's ID)
   123	    values   = [var.signer_web_identities[each.key].sub]
   124	  }
   125	  condition {
   126	    test     = "StringEquals"
   127	    variable = "accounts.google.com:oaud"         # the token's own aud
   128	    values   = [var.signer_web_identities[each.key].oaud]
   129	  }
   130	}
   131	# aws_iam_role.signer, data.aws_iam_policy_document.signer and aws_iam_role_policy.signer iterate over the same union.
   132	```
   133	
   134	With that change, `AWS_ROLE_ARN` is `tessera-v1-issuer-signer-gpg`, `AWS_CHAIN_ROLE_ARN` is unset, and the
   135	federated role and `deploy.sh role` are not needed.
   136	
   137	## Protocol
   138	
   139	The issuer's side is authoritative: `service/issuer/tessera_issue/signers.py`, `SlotSigner.send` and the stand-in.
   140	`tests/test_issuer_protocol.py` drives this signer with the issuer's own `SlotSigner`. It builds requests inline and
   141	by reference, reads the responses, and verifies them with `SlotSigner.verify`.
   142	
   143	- Request, inline: `{"request_id", "sign_by", "frame_b64"}`.
   144	- Request, by reference: `{"request_id", "sign_by", "frame_ref", "frame_sha256"}`.
   145	- Response: `{"v": 1, "request_id", "sqs_message_id", "status": "SIGNED"|"REFUSED", "kind"?, "sig_b64"?,
   146	  "sig_sha256"?, "frame_sha256"?, "reason"?, "detail"?, "signer": {"slot": "gpg", "clock", "code_sha256",
   147	  "image", "key_version", "key_fp", "epsilon_s", "alg", "sign_until"}}`.
   148	- The issuer reads `request_id`, `status`, `sig_b64` and `reason`. The other fields are for logs and operators.
   149	
   150	Differences from the instrument's protocol, where the issuer's protocol governs:
   151	
   152	| # | Instrument (2026-10-14) | This signer | Why |
   153	|---|---|---|---|
   154	| 1 | Request members exactly `{request_id, frame_b64}` | Adds `sign_by` (**required**), and the reference shape `{frame_ref, frame_sha256}` | IS-03, IS-09. The issuer's `SlotSigner.send` allows `sign_by=None`, but its pipeline always sends one. A request with no deadline is refused, not signed at any later time |
   155	| 2 | Any base64 that `b64decode(validate=True)` accepts | Canonical base64 only | The issuer sends canonical base64. The verifier's `b64` is used |
   156	| 3 | `REQUEST_MALFORMED`, `REQUEST_TOO_LARGE` | `BAD_REQUEST`, with the detail in `detail` | The stand-in's reason |
   157	| 4 | — | New reasons: `SIGNING_STOPPED`, `FRAME_UNAVAILABLE`, `FRAME_DIGEST_MISMATCH` | IS-03, IS-09 |
   158	| 5 | `ALLOWED_ALGS` from the environment | The pair id, in code | The policy is code |
   159	| 6 | Codec: vendored P8 implementation A (gen.py) | The service verifier's codec | No `formal/` or `prototype/` code in the service. Refusal codes can be named differently |
   160	| 7 | Self-check: rule-O codec only | Codec, `sig_policy`, and verification on the frame (the issuer's checks) | The issuer would reject anything else as `bad-signature` |
   161	| 8 | Signature creation time: a separate `time.time()` | The same clock read as the stop check | So the creation time is never past `SIGN_UNTIL` or `sign_by` |
   162	| 9 | The same request twice in one run: signed twice | The second gets the first signature again | E-02: fewer orphan signatures |
   163	| 10 | Response `signer.allowed_algs` | `signer.alg`, `signer.slot` (the stand-in's), `signer.sign_until`, top-level `sig_sha256` | Logging |
   164	| 11 | Startup: name, algorithm, fingerprint | Also `protectionLevel == HSM` and the PEM's CRC32C | Stated custody |
   165	| 12 | Queues `tessera-slot2-*`, made by the instrument's script; role with SQS rights directly | The issuer's Terraform queues `tessera-v1-issuer-gpg-*`; role chained to the Terraform signer role (adds `s3:GetObject frames/*`) | One set of queues per slot |
   166	| 13 | Visibility timeout 60 s | 120 s (the issuer's Terraform default for request queues) | — |
   167	| 14 | Started by Cloud Scheduler, or `gcloud run jobs execute` from the client (E-01 fix) | Cloud Scheduler every minute. **The issuer's SNS wake topic for `gpg` has no subscriber** | See E-01 |
   168	
   169	The stand-in decodes a manifest frame without checking which key `sset[0]` names. This signer keeps the
   170	instrument's stricter check (`KEY_FP_MISMATCH`). The issuer's real manifests name the window's gpg key, so they pass.
   171	
   172	## E-01 and E-02 on this side
   173	
   174	- **E-01 (on-demand start).** The issuer publishes to the SNS topic `tessera-v1-issuer-gpg-wake`. A GCP job cannot
   175	  subscribe to SNS without a public HTTPS endpoint, so this signer does not try. Cloud Scheduler runs the job every
   176	  minute from window open to `SIGN_UNTIL`, and the scheduler is paused afterwards. The issuer's poller runs every
   177	  10 min, so a one-minute tick adds little latency. The cost is one short execution a minute, the instrument's
   178	  measured cost profile (`prototype/policy-signer/RESOURCES.md` G11). **Open:** whether the wake topic should get a
   179	  subscriber, or the issuer should stop publishing for `gpg`.
   180	- **E-02 (late and duplicate answers).** Every signature is logged to Cloud Logging (request id, frame SHA-256,
   181	  signature SHA-256) **before** its response is sent (`test_every_signature_is_logged_before_its_response`). If the
   182	  issuer retries with the same request id and frame inside one run, the retry gets the same signature
   183	  (`test_duplicate_request_in_one_run_gets_the_same_signature`). Across runs there is no memory. If a response was
   184	  sent but its delete failed, the next run signs again, and both responses carry the same request id and verify
   185	  (`test_redelivery_after_a_failed_delete_signs_again_in_a_later_run`). The issuer's drain records the second
   186	  response as a duplicate. A request whose `sign_by` has passed by the time a run reaches it is refused
   187	  (`test_late_request_past_sign_by_is_refused_not_signed`). That puts a time bound on any late signature.
   188	
   189	## Deploy
   190	
   191	Requires `gcloud` (logged in, project `tessera-slot2`) and the `tessera` AWS profile. Nothing below has been run.
   192	
   193	```bash
   194	cd service/signer-gcp && .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q          # green first
   195	export REQUEST_QUEUE_URL=...  RESPONSE_QUEUE_URL=...  FRAME_BUCKET=...   # terraform output signer_queues / result_bucket
   196	export SIGN_UNTIL=YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ                           # issuance-not-after minus 96 h
   197	./deploy.sh sas                 # runtime and scheduler service accounts
   198	./deploy.sh role                # AWS federated role (needs the runtime account's unique ID)
   199	#   then, in service/issuer/infra: signer_principals = { gpg = "<printed ARN>" }; terraform apply
   200	./deploy.sh keyiam              # cloudkms.signer + publicKeyViewer on slot2-spike for the runtime account only
   201	./deploy.sh registry            # artifact repo tessera-signer
   202	./deploy.sh build               # stages signer-gcp + verifier, Cloud Build, records .image-digest
   203	./deploy.sh job                 # Cloud Run job tessera-signer-gpg, image by digest
   204	./deploy.sh run-now             # one execution; its log must show "start" with the pinned key_fp, then "done"
   205	./deploy.sh scheduler           # every minute while signing is possible
   206	./deploy.sh harden              # re-check project and key IAM
   207	./deploy.sh retire-instrument   # delete slot2-signer job/scheduler and its key grants (requester check item 4)
   208	```
   209	
   210	## Tests
   211	
   212	```bash
   213	uv venv .venv --python 3.14 && uv pip install -p .venv/bin/python cryptography==50.0.2 pytest
   214	.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
   215	```
   216	
   217	`pyproject.toml` puts `../verifier` and `../issuer` on the test path. The issuer is used only by
   218	`test_issuer_protocol.py`. `test_independence.py` checks that the package imports nothing from `prototype/`,
   219	`formal/` or the issuer.
   220	
   221	## Not done, or not known
   222	
   223	- Nothing has run against real Cloud KMS, SQS, S3 or STS. The adapters are tested over fake transports only.
   224	- The `python:3.14-slim` base image is referenced by tag, not digest. It should be pinned by digest before the first
   225	  real build.
   226	- The policy does not pin the frame's `issuer` domain, or the manifest's `key_fp` (the KMS slot's fingerprint). A
   227	  captor of the queue could get the gpg key's possession signature over a manifest that pairs it with a KMS key of
   228	  its own. Whether that is within C3 as registered is for the owner to judge. Configuration could narrow it.
   229	- No review has been run. A Codex review and an owner read come before deployment.
